{"records":[{"id":"cj2x496wp4","title":"Supplementary data files for the research paper entitled \"The evolution of fangs, venom and mimicry systems in blenny fishes\"","description":"There are three data files that relate to the paper entitled \"The evolution of fangs, venom and mimicry systems in blenny fishes\". Files S1 and S2 are fasta files that contain the assembled contigs from the Meiacanthus atrodorsalis venom gland transcriptome (File S1) and the Plagiotremus tapeinosoma control transcriptome (File S2). File S3 is an excel spreadsheet that contains the proteomic data generated from Meiacanthus grammistes venom and the results of its annotation with sequence databases and the translated transcriptome datasets.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-03-09T09:38:22.657Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cj2x496wp4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cj2x496wp4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Nicholas","last_name":"Casewell","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics","Transcriptomics"],"journal_issn":["09609822"]},{"id":"wy5ytmr2g5","title":"Protective effect of epigallocatechin-3-gallate (EGCG) on toxic metalloproteinases-mediated skin damage induced by Scyphozoan jellyfish envenomation","description":"Jellyfish stingings are currently raising serious public health concerns around the world. Therefore, the search of novel first-aid reagent suitable for envenomations from wide ranging stinging jellyfish species has become important. Previous in vitro and in vivo reports demonstrated that the metalloproteinase activity of N. Nomurai venom (NnV) was the major contributor to envenomation pathology. Therefore, metalloproteinase inhibitors make a promising candidate for the treatment of Scyphozoan jellyfish envenomation. Plant polyphenols have shown potential as treatments for neutralizing snake venoms and toxins. The major polyphenol component in green tea, Epigallocatechin-3-gallate (EGCG), has demonstrated previously metalloproteinase inhibition and we explore its potential as a NnV treatment. We show that EGCG inhibits the proteolytic activity of NnV as demonstrated by gelatin zymography. EGCG reduced NnV-induced cell death of HaCaT and HDF cells combined with their secretions of human matrix metalloproteinase (MMP)-2 and -9. Simulated rat NnV envenomation study showed that topical treatments with EGCG significantly ameliorated the progression of the dermonecrotic lesion typically induced by NnV. EGCG also reduced the activities of tissue MMP-2 and MMP-9, which play crucial role in pathogenesis of NnV. Therefore, we proposed that EGCG might be an effective therapeutic agent for the treatment of cutaneoous jellyfish symptoms.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-24T06:24:03.716Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wy5ytmr2g5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wy5ytmr2g5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Euikyung","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Jellyfish"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ngr4mnf3kt","title":"Data for: Comparative proteomics of geographically distinct saw-scaled viper (Echis carinatus) venoms from India","description":"We investigated on venom variation in geographically distinct populations of Echis carinatus from three ecologically distinct regions: Tamil Nadu (ECVTN), Goa (ECVGO), and Rajasthan (ECVRAJ). Venom was fractionated by RP-HPLC, combined with SDS-PAGE, and subjected to tandem mass spectrometry. Toxins were identified, and their relative abundance was estimated. Using NCBI database of Echis genus, we queried the MS/MS spectra, and found 87, 44 and 48 proteins, in ECVTN, ECVGO and ECVRAJ respectively, belonging to 7-9 different toxin families. The differences in the venom profiles were due to change in the relative composition of the toxin families.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-11T07:18:50.842Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ngr4mnf3kt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ngr4mnf3kt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Karthikeyan","last_name":"Vasudevan","orcid":null},{"first_name":" Siddarth","last_name":"Bhatia","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics","Venom"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nwd9m2t8v5","title":"Data for: Comparative proteomics of geographically distinct saw-scaled viper (Echis carinatus) venoms from India","description":"We investigated on venom variation in geographically distinct populations of Echis carinatus from three ecologically distinct regions: Tamil Nadu (ECVTN), Goa (ECVGO), and Rajasthan (ECVRAJ). Venom was fractionated by RP-HPLC, combined with SDS-PAGE, and subjected to tandem mass spectrometry. Toxins were identified, and their relative abundance was estimated. Using NCBI database of Echis genus, we queried the MS/MS spectra, and found 87, 44 and 48 proteins, in ECVTN, ECVGO and ECVRAJ respectively, belonging to 7-9 different toxin families. The differences in the venom profiles were due to change in the relative composition of the toxin families. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-06-23T15:10:22.532Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nwd9m2t8v5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nwd9m2t8v5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Karthikeyan","last_name":"Vasudevan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mass Spectrometry","Snake","Reverse Phase High Performance Liquid Chromatography","Envenomation","Proteome Analysis","Venom"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cpnscw2gkc","title":"Venom Sampling via Trivial Centrifugation","description":"This is the supporting raw data and information behind the published paper \"Speedy milking of fresh venom from Aculeate hymenopterans\" by Fox et al. (2018), available at: \nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0041010118300990\n\nIt includes:\n- Draft version of the manuscript prior to submission;\n- Peer review details;\n- Raw chromatograms obtained for centrifuge-milked venoms of fire ants, two species of bees, one species of Asian hornet;\n- Raw chromatograms of internal and calibration standards;\n- Images and details on the identification of utilised insects;\n- Pictures obtained for the venoms extracted and utilised structures.\n\nDisclaimer:\nThe presented method for venom extraction is extremely simple, and can be extended to other poisonous animal through simple adaptations. Such secretions can be highly dangerous. Responsible use of this technology is strongly recommended. Venom obtained via such method is to be used for legal, surveilled scientific purposes **only**. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-03-05T12:45:59.914Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cpnscw2gkc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cpnscw2gkc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Eduardo","last_name":"Fox","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Meng","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lei","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Li","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yongyue","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences","Zoology","Entomology","Chromatography","Chemical Ecology","Gas Chromatography","Sample Extraction","Animal Science","Medical Biology","Enzyme Secretion","Bioassay","Analytical Method","Toxins","Poison","Centrifugation","Poisonous Residues","Ant","Bug","Toxinology"],"journal_issn":["00410101"]},{"id":"5hb8cpbfky","title":"Supplementary material_Head and venom gland morphology of the common lancehead Bothrops atrox vary between specimens and different habitats in the Western Amazon, Brazil","description":"These are data analyzed in the research on Head and venom gland morphology of the common lancehead Bothrops atrox vary between specimens and different habitats in the Western Amazon, Brazil.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-07-17T06:13:44.431Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5hb8cpbfky","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5hb8cpbfky"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Samuel","last_name":"Garcia da Silva","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zs9jf6xc78","title":"Safety evaluation of the venom from scorpion Rhopalurus junceus: assessment of oral short term, subchronic toxicity and teratogenic effect","description":"Plot data of the manuscript Safety evaluation of the venom from scorpion Rhopalurus junceus: assessment of oral short term, subchronic toxicity and teratogenic effect","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-01-28T10:08:48.697Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zs9jf6xc78","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zs9jf6xc78"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alicia","last_name":"Lagarto","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Scatter Plot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vfn23bcx48","title":"Allergenic Proteins from Snake Venoms","description":"An occupational allergy study was conducted with venom and snake handlers from an intensive captive facility to assess whether these handlers had become sensitized over time in their work environment. This study allowed us to identify the main toxins in the venoms of Bothrops jararaca and Crotalus durissus terrificus responsible for sensitizing four handlers working with these captive animals.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-11-27T07:44:07.792Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vfn23bcx48","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vfn23bcx48"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lucilene Delazari dos","last_name":"Santos","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mass Spectrometry","Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"g6xz3j6hp8","title":"Proteomics of venoms of Naja naja and Daboia russelii snakes","description":"The current research advises the usages of variations in proteomics of venoms of Naja naja and Daboia russelii for identification of suspected snake venom samples seized by law enforcement agencies across the world, especially in India. Illegal trade of endangered species of snakes and their venoms is prohibited by Indian Wildlife Protection Act, 1972. There are provisions of fines and imprisonments if a person is found involved in such illegal activities. The Naja naja and Daboia russelii, both are endangered species and illegal trading of venoms of both these snakes is frequently encountered by various law enforcement agencies. But to frame charges under Wildlife Protection Law, the enforcement agencies are required to prove beyond any doubt that the seized material is venom illegally milked from these species. The unique proteomes of venoms can be helpful in confirm identification of snake species from which the venom is likely milked.\nThe data is generated by LC-MS/MS (nano-Liquid Chromatography coupled with Q-Orbitrap mass spectrometr and ProteomeDiscoverer 2.2 software) of crude venoms of Naja naja and Daboia russelii.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-10-20T07:44:08.456Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/g6xz3j6hp8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/g6xz3j6hp8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Saurabh","last_name":"Bhargava","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rajvinder","last_name":"Singh","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Snake Venom Peptide","Forensic Science","Snake Venom","Wildlife Conservation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"m4ry5ry83h","title":"Data for: Safety evaluation of the venom from scorpion Rhopalurus junceus: assessment of oral short term, subchronic toxicity and teratogenic effect","description":"Data for short-term, subchronic toxicity and the teratogenic potential of Rhopalurus junceus scorpion venom by oral route in mice. Short-term oral toxicity was test in both sexes NMRI mice that received 100 mg/kg/day of scorpion venom extract for 28 days. For the subchronic study, mice were administered with three doses (0.1, 10, and 100 mg/kg) by oral route for 90 days. Teratogenic potential was tested in pregnant mice administered from day 6 to 15 post conception. Significant differences were observed in body weight and food intake of animal treated for short-term and subchronic assays. Variations in serum urea and cholesterol were observed after 90 days oral treatment. Spontaneous findings not related to the treatment were reveal in histology evaluation. Exposure in pregnant mice did not produce maternal toxicity. Signs of embryo-fetal toxicity were not observed. The current study provides evidence that exposure to low or moderate dose of Rhopalurus junceus scorpion venom by oral route did not affect health of animals and has low impact on reproductive physiology.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-03-31T15:03:42.484Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/m4ry5ry83h","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/m4ry5ry83h"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alicia","last_name":"Lagarto","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Toxicology","Teratogenesis","Venom"],"journal_issn":["00410101"]}],"total_records":122997,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":105,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":122997,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":122526,"restricted":61,"embargoed":410},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2059},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1336},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":553},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":442},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":418},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":387},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":387},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":342},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":274},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":246}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":120426},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1891},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":177},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":128},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":69},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":59},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":53},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":37},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Uni